Thank you very much Dennis.
Yes, that is the problem, the 32 combinations.  This was my best model according to AIC and I am trying to plot the results.
I will try your suggestion.
Best,

Manuel

On 11/10/2010 02:17 p.m., Dennis Murphy wrote:
> Hi:
>
> The problem is that with five factors each at two levels, there are 32 factor combinations. I suppose you could combine the levels of two factors twice to produce a 4 x 4 plot, and then make one plot with all 16 combinations at level 0 of the fifth factor and another at level 1 of the fifth factor. You could then use the 'paste two graphs on one page' trick using the ideas from section 8.4 of Hadley's book. I suspect that plot will be a bit cramped, but it's one approach to the problem...
>
> HTH,
> Dennis
>
> On Mon, Oct 11, 2010 at 10:10 AM, Manuel Spínola <mspin...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Thank you very much Rob.
> >
> > I will take a look at the rms.
> > I am following the idea of Andrew Gelman of plotting the model. instead of presenting a table with the coefficients, something that I do not see very often, at least in ecology.  Now, I found that having several categorical predictors, and even some of them with several levels, is challenging for showing the results, and even more challenging to make the plots.  I tried the effects package from John Fox, but the plot for categorical predictor is not what I would like for this type of variables.
> > Best,
> >
> > Manuel
> >
> > 2010/10/11 Rob James <r...@aetiologic.ca>
> >
> > > You might want to look at Prof Harrell's rms package which is a remarkable collection of regression tools reflecting his philosophy, including many graphical displays.
> > >
> > > It now (as of V3.0?) produces data frames from the Predict command and these df slide nicely into ggplot2.
> > >
> > > Harrell and Hadley are clearly friends and while Harrell's current rms package does not yet make (extensive use) of ggplot, I suspect that plans are afoot.
> > >
> > > I will say that have had less success (probably reflecting lack of effort) in converting Harrell's elegant bplot (heat-map) outputs into something equally elegant in ggplot2. But the fautl lies not in the software, but in the operator.  For displaying categorical predictors, etc, you can start with the rms standard outputs and then use the dataframes which get generated by Predict to feed ggplot. That said, rms has a very particular and specific take on things like bootstrapping, calibration, and to use it well really requires engaging with Harrell's larger methodological and statistical perspective.
> > >
> > >
> > >
> > > ---------------------------
> > > Robert C James, PhD
> > >
> > > On Mon 11/10/10 05:33 , Manuel Spínola mspin...@gmail.com sent:
> > > > Thank you very much Andrie,
> > > >
> > > > Something like you have done with facet will be very good.  I was thinking also on a plot like the result from a cluster analysis or a classification tree where the end will be a point range display showing the point estimate and the 95% CI (the predicted probabilities).
> > > > Yes, I agree with you, I used number to facilitate the process but the 1 and 0 are basically presence - absence for each categorical predictor.
> > > > I am trying to plot the result of a logistic model when your predictors are categorical. I have plotting without any problem the result of a model when variables are continuous using stat_smooth, but I have trouble to express graphically the model for categorical predictors.
> > > >
> > > > Best,
> > > >
> > > > Manuel
