From u5196f936ff792c35 at groups.google.com  Sat Oct 30 09:23:04 2010
From: u5196f936ff792c35 at groups.google.com (Brandon Hurr)
Date: Sat, 30 Oct 2010 09:23:04 +0000
Subject: Re: axis.title.x = theme_blank() not working
Message-ID: <D8IGV9l4pjQ.Lb5LF4HMnJEJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <D8IGV9l4pjQ.ojKiC7ehS14J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <D8IGV9l4pjQ.Vd-ViHqA5iMJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com> <D8IGV9l4pjQ.ojKiC7ehS14J@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/D8IGV9l4pjQ/m/Lb5LF4HMnJEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I don't think it was coord_flip(), I think it was theme_bw() which has defaults for axis.x.text that are black. When you set your axis text with opts to theme_blank() and then had the call for theme_bw() afterwards it uses the last call to set the theme. At least that's my experience. Like you said Ista, putting your opts() call at the end fixes it.

B

On Thu, Oct 28, 2010 at 17:39, Ista Zahn <iz...@psych.rochester.edu> wrote:
> Hi Manuel,
>
> On Thu, Oct 28, 2010 at 11:52 AM, Manuel Spínola <mspin...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > Dear list members,
> >
> > I am trying to put 2 different plots in one page (p1 and p2):
> >
> > a= rnorm(20, 5.35, 1.6)
> > b = rnorm(20, 3.5, 1.1)
> > grupo = rep(c("a", "b"), c(20, 20))
> > cont = c(a,b)
> > cont
> > df = data.frame(cont, grupo)
> >
> > sea = sd(a)/sqrt(20)
> > seb = sd(b)/sqrt(20)
> >
> > dfplot = data.frame(sexo = factor(c("Machos", "Hembras"), levels=c("Machos",
> > "Hembras")),
> >  punto = c(mean(a), mean(b)),
> >  ymin = c(mean(a) - 2*sea, c(mean(b) - 2*sea)),
> >  ymax = c(mean(a) + 2*seb, c(mean(b) + 2*sea)))
> > dfplot
> >
> > pa = ggplot(dfplot, aes(x = sexo, y = punto, ymin= ymin, ymax= ymax, colour
> > = sexo))
> > p1 = pa + geom_pointrange(size = 0.8) + xlab("Sexo") + ylab("Concentración
> > contaminante (ppm)") + scale_y_continuous(limits=c(0,6)) + theme_bw() +
> > opts(legend.position = "none")
> >
> > dfdif = data.frame(
> >    dif= 2,
> >    punto = 1.72,
> >    ymin = 1.06,
> >    ymax = 2.39)
> >
> > pb = ggplot(dfdif, aes(x = dif, y = punto, ymin= ymin, ymax= ymax, colour =
> > "red"))
> > p2 = pb + geom_pointrange(size = 0.8) + opts(axis.title.x = theme_blank()) +
> > geom_hline(yintercept=0.0, colour = "green", size = 1, linetype = 2) +
> > coord_flip()  + ylab("Diferencia entre machos y hembras (ppm)") + theme_bw()
> > + scale_y_continuous(limits=c(0,3)) + opts(legend.position = "none")
> >
> > I have 2 questions:
> >
> > Is the procedure in Hadley's book the only way to put 2 plots in a page?
> > If that is a yes, How can I enlarge the window (with) to have a better
> > appearance of the plots?
>
> An alternative is
>
> install.packages("ggExtra", repos="http://R-Forge.R-project.org")
> library(ggExtra)
> align.plots(p1, p2)
>
> >
> > In the second plot: "opts(axis.title.x = theme_blank())" is not working,
> > why?
>
> It's getting confused by coord_flip. Putting the opts() call at the end works:
>
> p2 = pb + geom_pointrange(size = 0.8) + geom_hline(yintercept=0.0,
> colour = "green", size = 1, linetype = 2) + coord_flip()  +
> ylab("Diferencia entre machos y hembras (ppm)") + theme_bw() +
> scale_y_continuous(limits=c(0,3)) + opts(axis.title.x = theme_blank(),
> legend.position = "none")
>
> -Ista
